NNAAMMEE
       pbmtog3 - convert a PBM image into a Group 3 MH fax file


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppbbmmttoogg33 [--rreevveerrsseebbiittss] [--nnooffiixxeeddwwiiddtthh] [--aalliiggnn88|--aalliiggnn1166] [_p_b_m_f_i_l_e]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppbbmmttoogg33  reads  a PBM image as input and produces a Group 3 MH fax file
       as output.

       You can also generate a TIFF file that uses the same  encoding  inside,
       with ppaammttoottiiffff.

       There is no program in Netpbm that generates other fax formats, such as
       MR and MMR, but ppaammttoottiiffff can generate TIFF files that use those encod‐
       ings.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ),  ppbbmmttoogg33  recognizes  the
       following command line options:



       --rreevveerrsseebbiittss
              This option causes the output to have the bits in every byte re‐
              versed so the least significant bit becomes the most significant
              bit.   Some  fax  modems  expect bits in reverse order, and this
              compensates for that. If you get a  whole  bunch  of  "bad  code
              word"  messages  when  you  try  to  read the G3 file (e.g. with
              gg33ttooppbbmm), try using this option.  Note that the output is not G3
              when you use this option.


       --nnooffiixxeeddwwiiddtthh
              Most  fax  machines expect the image to be 1728 columns wide, so
              ppbbmmttoogg33 cuts the output to this width by default.  If  you  want
              to keep the width of the original image, use this option.

              This  option  was  new in Netpbm 10.6 (July 2002).  Before that,
              ppbbmmttoogg33 always kept the width of the original image.


       --aalliiggnn88

       --aalliiggnn1166
              These options say to align output rows to 8-bit or 16-bit bound‐
              aries,  respectively,  by  adding  padding  bits in front of EOL
              codes

              Without these options, ppbbmmttoogg33 adds no padding and rows may  be‐
              gin and ends anywhere within a byte.

              You cannot specify both.

              These options were new in Netpbm 10.79 (June 2017).




